<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1"
          xmlns:asmv3="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">

  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="LuminaMonitor.App" />

  <!--
    Per-monitor DPI awareness, and it matters here for a reason beyond tidiness.

    Without it WPF is system-DPI aware: it draws at the primary monitor's
    scaling and lets Windows stretch the result to whatever the actual monitor
    uses. On this machine the primary screen runs at 100 % and the one the app
    lives on at 150 %, so a window the application believed was 563 x 1194
    arrived on screen as 845 x 1791 pixels — 351 pixels taller than the 1440-px
    display, with the bottom simply gone.

    The picture paid for it too. The video was decoded, scaled to 1080 units,
    rasterised, and only then blown up by half again by the compositor: a
    bitmap stretch of an already-flattened image, which is exactly the blur
    the mirror is supposed to avoid. Per-monitor awareness hands WPF the real
    pixel grid, so the frame is scaled once, from the source, at the size it
    will actually be shown.
  -->
  <asmv3:application>
    <asmv3:windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </asmv3:windowsSettings>
  </asmv3:application>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

</assembly>
